   232	**Launcher** (a copy of `results/pods/reset_pod.sh` with `podctl.py`, unchanged pod safety; draft
   233	`docs/revision/churn/churn_pod.sh`, installed in `results/pods/` after review): code and tarball guards; creation only
   234	before the hard deadline; the price checked against the ceiling `MAXCPH`; the memory floor (28 GiB); the gate, then the
   235	stages, each started only if no STOP file exists and the soft deadline has not passed (the soft deadline is the cap at
   236	the ceiling, net of earlier attempts' charges); at the soft deadline it writes STOP, so the pod stops after the running
   237	stage. **The one exception to "no running stage is killed":** the `podctl.py` watchdog deletes the pod at 1.3 times the
   238	cap at the price ceiling (10.40 USD at most for this arm), also mid-stage, as an absolute guard against a hung pod. Spend
   239	(amended 2026-11-12, launcher review r1) is recorded as the account balance difference (`podctl.py spend NAME`, at
   240	least an hour after the watchdog ends); the launcher prints only estimates. Its attempts file holds each pod's id,
   241	request time and deletion time. The launcher passes to the driver the live pod's request time as `--started`, its
   242	actual price, and as `--prior-usd` the operator's `PRIOR` (recorded spend of earlier launches of this arm) plus this
   243	launch's deleted attempts charged at the ceiling from their request times, rounded up. Both deadlines are net of
   244	`PRIOR`, so the 10.40 USD bound holds across launches. An EXIT, INT or TERM trap pulls the records, verifies them against the inventories, deletes the pod, and
   245	verifies the deletion; the watchdog keeps deleting until no pod of this name remains.
